<h2>The suit and the family behind it</h2>
<p>The plaintiff, owner of a house in Pandav Nagar, Delhi, sued for possession of its ground floor, occupied by his widowed daughter-in-law and her three children after his son&rsquo;s death. The trial court decreed the suit in April 2024: the defendants had not proved the property was ancestral, the licence under which they occupied it had been terminated by a legal notice of December 2016, and pending DV Act proceedings were no bar. The widow appealed under Section 96 CPC.</p>

<h2>Three questions, three answers</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Is it a shared household?</strong> Yes. She lived there in a domestic relationship — with her husband, and with her father-in-law as a member of his family. Section 2(s) does not require her to hold any title, and Satish Chander Ahuja settled that even self-acquired property of in-laws can be a shared household. Her husband&rsquo;s death did not extinguish the right, which Section 17(1) confers on every woman in a domestic relationship.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Can the owner still get a possession decree?</strong> Yes. Section 17(2) protects against eviction &ldquo;save in accordance with the procedure established by law&rdquo; — and a properly instituted civil suit is exactly that procedure. The right of residence is statutory but not indefeasible, particularly where the relationship has broken down and complaints run in both directions.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>What must accompany the decree?</strong> A balancing exercise the trial court skipped. Following the approach approved by the Supreme Court in Ravneet Kaur v. Prithpal Singh Dhingra, execution was deferred until alternate accommodation of a comparable level is secured, or rent of Rs 25,000 per month is paid.</div>
</div>

<h2>Where HAMA entered the picture</h2>
<p>What distinguishes this case from the usual daughter-in-law versus in-laws litigation is the widowhood. Ordinarily, the obligation to provide a wife residence rests on the husband. Here the husband had died, so the Court turned to Section 19 of the Hindu Adoptions and Maintenance Act, 1956, under which a widowed daughter-in-law unable to maintain herself from her own earnings, property or her husband&rsquo;s estate is entitled to maintenance from her father-in-law. Read with Section 3(b) — which defines maintenance to include residence — the father-in-law&rsquo;s ownership victory carried a statutory duty with it.</p>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Provision</th><th>What it does in this setting</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 2(s), DV Act</td><td>Makes the in-laws&rsquo; house a shared household where the woman lived in a domestic relationship, regardless of title</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 17(2), DV Act</td><td>Permits eviction only through procedure established by law — which includes a civil possession suit</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 19, HAMA</td><td>Obliges the father-in-law to maintain a widowed daughter-in-law who cannot maintain herself</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Section 3(b), HAMA</td><td>Defines maintenance to include provision for residence</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The respondent pointed to about Rs 25 lakh the family had received in 2016 in execution of an arbitration award in the deceased husband&rsquo;s favour. The Court treated the receipt as relevant but not decisive: a sum of that size, received a decade ago and meant to support a family of four, could not reasonably secure a home for life.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for practice</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p><strong>For owners:</strong> the possession suit remains maintainable, but plead and prove what alternate arrangement is offered. An unconditional decree against a woman with a residence right is vulnerable on appeal.</p>
  <p><strong>For widowed daughters-in-law:</strong> the shared-household right survives the husband&rsquo;s death, and HAMA Section 19 supplies an independent maintenance anchor — but neither confers ownership, and a court can relocate the right into alternate accommodation or rent.</p>
  <p><strong>For both sides:</strong> the figure the owner himself pleads as mesne profits or damages can become the benchmark for the rent he must pay. Pleadings have consequences.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This note summarises a judgment of the Delhi High Court for general information. It is not legal advice; outcomes in possession and residence disputes turn on the specific record.</div>
